Blind spot: reconcile commits written after each landing are not simulated and can add conflicts
Blind spot: a B1 repair pushes a new head, which must pass its checks again before it lands (it defers if they do not)
Blind spot: standalone mode: there is no B1 (no ledger), so every conflicting path needs review and a conflicting PR is handed off, never repaired
Blind spot: stacked members #106/#107: their branches share commits that are not on the base. After the earlier one is squash-merged, GitHub can mark the later one CONFLICTING even though merge-tree here is clean, and re-setting the PR base does not clear it; merge the base into the later branch (new head, fresh checks, re-pin)

Forecast advice, not a decision. To act on it at --execute, state your own reason:
  --landing-plan group --landing-reason "<your reason>"

Queue holds 16 PRs. GH-1066 requires an explicit decision under --execute:
  --landing-plan direct|group --landing-reason "<justification>"
  • direct: Sequential landing on development (best for small/independent PRs).
  • group:  Combine candidates into a single integration branch to minimize gate churn.
